// runtime/sfn/platform/fd_io.sfn
//
// Generic-fd `read`/`write`, isolated behind two symbols so the Windows
// divergence is expressible as a module swap. Swapped for
// `fd_io_windows.sfn` on a Windows target by
// `target_condition_runtime_sfn_sources`
// (`compiler/src/build/target.sfn`) — the same mechanism that swaps
// `fs_exec_mode.sfn`, and for the same reason (SFN-671).
//
// This module is deliberately separate from `socket_ops.sfn`, which
// fixes the identical `int`-vs-`ssize_t` width defect for `recv`/`send`
// (SFN-927). The split is a separation of concerns: generic-fd I/O and
// Winsock socket I/O are distinct handle spaces (see below), and the
// POSIX `socket_ops.sfn` is genuinely socket-only, so an ungated
// consumer like `io.sfn` or `assert.sfn` has no business importing it.
//
// The link-time argument for the split is real, but it is
// target-dependent, so state it precisely rather than either way round.
//
// On Linux and macOS the `net` gate (`runtime/capsule.toml`
// `[sfn-source-gates]`) does narrow: a binary that never declares
// `![net]` does not link `socket_ops.sfn`, so an ungated consumer
// reaching for `sfn_fd_read`/`sfn_fd_write` there would leave them
// undefined at link. That is not hypothetical — it is exactly the
// failure the SFN-927 fixture manifests produced when `fd_io.sfn` was
// added to `io.sfn`'s call path but omitted from their `sfn-sources`
// lists: `ld.lld: error: undefined symbol: sfn_fd_write, referenced by
// io.sfn`.
//
// On the msvc target the same argument does NOT hold, and must not be
// relied on: the gate fails open there. `compute_runtime_demand`
// (`build/runtime_selection.sfn`) short-circuits to "retain everything"
// when `runtime_link_dead_strips()` is false, and that predicate tests
// `target_uses_gnu_link_gc`, false for the msvc ABI. Measured on a plain
// `![io]` hello-world with `SAILFIN_TRACE_LINK=1`:
// `socket_ops_windows.sfn`'s object is on the link line, along with
// every other `net`-gated module, and what removes the unreferenced code
// is the linker's dead-strip (`-ffunction-sections` + `/OPT:REF`), not
// the effect system. Tracked as SFN-943.
//
// So: the split is right everywhere, load-bearing on Linux and macOS,
// and merely tidy on msvc. Do not simplify this to one target's answer —
// an earlier revision of this comment asserted each half in turn, and
// both were wrong for the other target.
//
// The UCRT's low-fd `_read`/`_write` (the CRT names — NOT usable on a
// Winsock `SOCKET`, which is a distinct handle space reached only
// through `recv`/`send`, see `socket_ops.sfn`) are `int` (32-bit), not
// POSIX's `ssize_t` (64-bit). A raw `-> i64` extern against them
// silently promotes a `-1` error into `4294967295`, defeating every
// `if n <= 0` / `if n < 0` guard downstream — the identical defect
// `socket_ops.sfn` fixes for `recv`/`send` (SFN-927).
//
// On POSIX both are pass-throughs, so this target's behaviour is
// unchanged.
extern fn read(fd: i32, buf: *u8, count: usize) -> i64;

extern fn write(fd: i32, buf: *u8, count: usize) -> i64;

fn sfn_fd_read(fd: i32, buf: *u8, count: usize) -> i64 {
    return read(fd, buf, count);
}

fn sfn_fd_write(fd: i32, buf: *u8, count: usize) -> i64 {
    return write(fd, buf, count);
}

export { sfn_fd_read, sfn_fd_write };
